{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Anemic Domain Model.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Anemic Domain Model.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"anemic domain model anemic domain model","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Anemic Domain Model.md","fileName":"Anemic Domain Model.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Anemic Domain Model","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-22 20:53\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Domain Model Building Blocks]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Domain Model Building Blocks","Domain Model","Entity Invariant","hands-ondomain-drivendesignwithnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Anemic Domain Model.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Anemic Domain Model.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"anemic domain model anemic domain model","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Anemic Domain Model.md","fileName":"Anemic Domain Model.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Anemic Domain Model","heading":"Anemic Domain Model","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An anemic domain model contains data structures and relationships but leaves most behavior elsewhere, often in services, SQL, or database procedures. It can closely mirror a persistence schema while revealing little about what the system actually does. A data model is appropriate when persistence is the only need, but calling it a [[Domain Model]] obscures the absence of domain behavior. Behavior-rich entities instead keep state transitions and [[Entity Invariant]]s near the state they protect.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Domain Model Building Blocks","Domain Model","Entity Invariant","hands-ondomain-drivendesignwithnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Anemic Domain Model.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Anemic Domain Model.md","sou